course: world
lesson: 16
title: Climate zones
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Weather changes from day to day, but climate is the usual weather of a place, measured over many years.
explain: Near the equator the Sun's rays strike the ground most directly, so it is hot all year; near the poles the rays come in at a low slant and spread their heat thin, so it is cold.
explain: So the Earth has broad belts called climate zones: hot tropical zones near the equator, cold polar zones near the poles, and temperate zones in between with four seasons.
explain: Mountains and seas change the climate too: it gets colder the higher you climb, and places by the sea have milder winters and cooler summers than places far inland.
explain: For example, Ukraine lies in the temperate zone, with snowy winters and warm summers, while the Amazon rainforest near the equator is hot and wet all year.
fact: Weather is what is happening now; climate is the usual weather over many years.
fact: The tropics near the equator are hot all year because the Sun's rays fall on them most directly.
fact: The polar zones are cold all year because the Sun's rays strike them at a low slant.
fact: The temperate zones lie between the tropics and the poles and have four seasons.
fact: A desert is a place that gets very little rain or snow; it can be hot like the Sahara or cold like Antarctica.
fact: The air gets colder the higher you climb, so high peaks can have snow even near the equator.
fact: The sea warms up and cools down slowly, so it makes the climate of coasts milder.
fact: Plants and animals are adapted to their climate, like polar bears to the Arctic and camels to the desert.
fact: A camel can go many days without drinking and copes with great heat; its hump stores fat, not water.
fact: The seasons come from the tilt of the Earth's axis, not from the Earth being nearer to or farther from the Sun.
exercise: What is climate? | The usual weather of a place over many years | The weather that you can see outside the window today | The temperature at noon | The rain from one storm
exercise: Why is it hot all year near the equator? | The Sun's rays strike it most directly | It is much closer to the Sun than other places | The ground there makes its own heat | There are never any clouds there
exercise: Why are the polar zones cold? | Sunlight strikes them at a low slant and spreads thin | They are much farther away from the Sun than the rest of the Earth | The ice there puts the sunlight out | There is no air at the poles
exercise: Which zone has four clear seasons? | The temperate zone | The tropical zone | The polar zone | Every zone equally
exercise: What makes a place a desert? | It gets very little rain or snow | It is always very, very hot there | It is covered all over in golden sand | It has no animals or plants at all
exercise: Why can there be snow on top of a mountain near the equator? | The air gets colder the higher you go | The top of the mountain is nearer the pole | Snow is blown there from the poles | Mountains make their own snow from rock
exercise: Why do towns by the sea often have milder winters than towns far inland? | The sea cools down slowly and keeps the coast warmer | Sea air never has any clouds | The crashing waves make heat | Salt in the sea air stops any frost from forming there
exercise: In which climate zone does Ukraine lie? | Temperate | Tropical | Polar | Hot desert
exercise: Which animal is adapted to a polar climate? | Polar bear | Desert camel | Green parrot | River crocodile
exercise: What causes the seasons? | The tilt of the Earth's axis | The Earth getting nearer to and farther from the Sun | The Moon covering part of the Sun | The Sun getting hotter and colder
exercise: Which place is hot and wet all year? | A tropical rainforest | The frozen Arctic Ocean | A cool temperate forest | Icy Antarctica in the south
exercise: Is Antarctica a desert? | Yes, because very little snow or rain falls there | No, because it is so cold | No, because it has no sand | Yes, because it is really hot down under the thick ice
exercise: Which sentence describes climate rather than weather? | Summers here are usually hot and dry | It is very windy and rainy this afternoon | It snowed last night | Tomorrow will be cloudy
exercise: How do camels manage in the hot desert? | Their bodies are adapted to heat and little water | They drink seawater from the coast | They sleep through the whole summer | They store all their water inside their big humps
test: A traveller goes from the equator to the North Pole. What does she find on the way? | Hot lands, then lands with four seasons, then icy lands | Icy lands all the way | Lands that get hotter and hotter the farther north she travels | The same climate everywhere
test: Which is true? | One cold day does not change a place's climate | One rainy week turns a place into a rainforest | A place's climate changes every morning | Weather and climate mean the same thing
test: A torch shining straight down makes a small bright spot; shining at a slant, a big dim patch. What does this show about the Earth? | Slanting sunlight spreads its heat thin, as near the poles | The poles are farther away from the Sun than the equator is | A torch is hotter than the Sun | Light gets brighter when it slants
test: Two towns are equally far from the equator; one is on the coast and one is far inland. Which usually has colder winters? | The town far inland | The town on the coast | They must have the same winters | The one with more trees
test: A climber in Africa reaches snow near the top of a mountain. Why is there snow? | It is much colder high up the mountain | She has walked towards the South Pole | All of Africa has snowy winters | Sunlight cannot reach mountain tops
test: When it is summer in Ukraine, what season is it in Australia? | Winter, because Australia is in the southern half of the Earth | Also summer, because it is the same planet | Spring, because Australia is ahead in time | Autumn, because Australia is on the other side of the whole world
test: Which place is a cold desert? | Antarctica | The Amazon rainforest | A misty forest on the coast | A green rainy island
test: Why would a polar bear struggle to live in a tropical forest? | It is adapted to cold, not to heat | It cannot see well in green light | It needs salt water to drink | It can only walk on sand
test: Which best describes the temperate zone? | Warm summers, cold winters and four seasons | Hot and wet all year round | Frozen all year round | Hot and dry all year round
test: whats the diff between weather and climate | Weather is now, climate is the usual weather over many years | Weather is rain, climate is sunshine | They mean exactly the same thing | Climate is today, and weather is the usual weather over many years
test: how come there's snow on mountains near the equator | The air gets colder the higher you climb | Snow falls only near the equator | The mountains are closer to the Moon | Mountains make their own ice inside
test: my cousin says deserts are always hot, so Antarctica can't be one. true? | No, a desert is a place with very little rain or snow, hot or cold | Yes, deserts must be hot and sandy | Yes, Antarctica has far too much ice and snow to be a desert at all | No, Antarctica is a desert because it's flat
test: Tom says the coast is colder in winter than inland cos of the sea. right? | No, the sea cools slowly and usually makes coasts milder | Yes, the sea always makes winters colder | Yes, the sea freezes the air near it | No, the coast is colder because of the sand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises climate? | The usual weather of a place over many years | The state of the air at a given moment | The temperature recorded on a given day | The quantity of rain in a single storm
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the tropics remain hot throughout the year. | The rays of the Sun strike them most directly | They lie nearest to the Sun in distance | They receive heat from the Earth's interior | They are surrounded by warm oceans alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning deserts. | A desert is defined by scarcity of precipitation, and may be hot or cold | A desert is invariably hot and sandy | A desert is any region devoid of animals | A desert is any region lying upon the equator
exam: Identify the principal reason for which lofty peaks near the equator may bear snow. | The air becomes colder with increasing altitude | The equator receives snowfall throughout the year | The Sun does not shine upon mountains | Mountains are nearer to the poles
